      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The strength of each frequency component is determined by the **amplitude** of the corresponding sine wave in the decomposition. When you perform the Fourier Transform, you are essentially projecting your original messy signal onto a set of infinite, pure sine waves of different frequencies.\n\nThink of it like shining a light through a prism. The \"loudness\" or \"strength\" of a specific frequency is simply how much of that specific pure sine wave is required to perfectly reconstruct the original signal. If a frequency is \"loud\" in your original signal, it means a very large amplitude sine wave of that specific frequency is needed to build it back up. If it's \"quiet,\" only a tiny wiggle of that frequency is needed.\n\nMathematically, this comes down to the **inner product** (or correlation) between your signal and the basis sine wave. You calculate how much the signal \"aligns\" with that specific frequency. If they align perfectly (constructive interference), the result is a large number (high amplitude). If they are out of phase or don't match well, the result is small or zero.\n\nSo, the graph you see in the frequency domain is literally a map of these alignment scores. The height of the bar at",
